00:00:02.389 --> 00:00:05.030 align:start position:0%
leaders of the nato alliance will meet  in madrid next week a pivotal moment for  european security under the shadow of  russia's war in ukraine  images like these will doubtless be on  their mind the depravity unleashed by  russian forces on the ukrainian town of  butcher has prompted some allies to say  they cannot let a butcher-style atrocity  happen on nato soil  this will mean the biggest overhaul in  nato's defense and deterrence posture  since the cold war aimed at denying  russia the ability to invade  such a move looks set to include the  expansion of a 40 000 strong force on a  higher level of readiness possibly by  six times that number according to two  military sources it's  really important to be able to signal as  a country and as an alliance that yes  you russia can try to attack us and you  may even proceed and attack us but we'll  make sure that you get far less after  the out of the attack than you were  planning  on getting  also part of this new policy will be the  strengthening of eight nato units  already deployed to deter russian  attacks on the alliance's eastern and  south eastern flanks these include the  three baltic states poland romania and  others they could be grown by thousands  of troops but in a key change many of  these new soldiers could be based at  home for example in the uk and be  pre-assigned to a country if needed  it's not just about troop numbers more  military equipment like armored vehicles  possibly air defense systems and other  heavy weapons could be pre-positioned in  front-line baltic states it's a very  opportune moment  and also very crucial moment for the  alliance to stick together to to show  that this is the strongest alliance in  the world not only by words but also by  deeds as well as galvanizing member  states vladimir putin's war has prompted  historically neutral sweden and finland  to ask to join nato  this should be the ultimate signal of  unity that allies could send to the  kremlin but turkey has so far held up  the celebrations because of concerns  about kurdish militants in sweden it's  not yet clear if last-ditch diplomatic  efforts will smooth over the rift in  time for madrid  what is certain though is that this  summit is nato's moment to reshape its  defenses and reduce the chance of  russia's invasion of ukraine escalating  into all-out war across europe
 

